WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.860
A speaking valve changes the route of exhaled air.

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:06.260
A tracheostomy provides an opening into the trachea through

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.620
the neck. Normally, exhaled air passes through the larynx,

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.000
where vibrating vocal folds produce sound. The valve opens

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.140
for inhalation through the tube. It closes during exhalation,

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.580
redirecting air around the tube and through the upper

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.160
airway. That redirected air can support voice.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:25.250
An exhalation route must remain open. The cuff must

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.310
be completely deflated before the valve is placed. An

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:31.030
inflated cuff can block the space needed for exhaled

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.290
air to pass around the tube, but deflating the

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.010
cuff is not enough by itself. The patient also

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:39.230
needs adequate upper airway airflow and must tolerate the

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.110
change. If airflow is inadequate, do not place the

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.110
valve. Further assessment is needed.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.240
The deck organizes evaluation into five steps. First, check

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.780
alertness, ability to follow directions, breathing comfort, and oxygen

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:57.760
stability. Second, assess secretions, cough, and clearance. Third, work

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.540
with respiratory therapy to deflate the cuff completely and

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.840
observe the response. Fourth, check airflow through the mouth

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.200
or nose and look for obstruction. Fifth, if appropriate,

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:11.200
try the valve under supervision while monitoring breathing, oxygen,

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:12.380
voice, and comfort.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.970
Audible speech does not override breathing difficulty. Remove the

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:20.950
valve for difficulty breathing, falling oxygen levels, increased effort,

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:25.930
stridor, anxiety, or distress, or inability to exhale. Document

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:30.370
the baseline cuff response, airflow, voice, and respiratory tolerance.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.910
The deck's successful example leads to initial speech -language

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:38.230
pathology and respiratory therapy supervision, followed by gradual progression

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:42.270
according to tolerance and facility protocol. That recommendation follows

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:45.350
the observed response. It is not automatic for every

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:45.690
patient.
